    {
      "id": "fnv55kj3f",
      "node_id": "node-clip-text-encode-(sdxl)-e87b6342",
      "version_tag": "v1",
      "title": "Clip Text Encode (SDXL)",
      "description": {
        "text": {
          "text": {
            "text": {
              "text": {
                "text": {
                  "text": {
                    "text": {
                      "text": "This node converts a text prompt into the dual-embedding format required by Stable Diffusion XL by leveraging both CLIP text encoders (CLIP-L and CLIP BigG). Since both encoders and their tokenizers are already preloaded by the wrapper, the node focuses on tokenization, encoding, and combining outputs into SDXL-compatible conditioning tensors.\n\nThe prompt is independently tokenized using each encoder’s attached tokenizer, with padding and truncation to the standard 77-token length. These tokens are then passed through their respective text encoders under autocast for efficient mixed-precision inference.\n\nFor SDXL conditioning, the node extracts the penultimate hidden states from both encoders and concatenates them along the feature dimension to form the final prompt_embeds. This provides richer semantic understanding compared to single-encoder setups. Additionally, it extracts a pooled embedding (from the [CLS] token of the second encoder), which is used for global conditioning in the diffusion process.\n\nThe outputs are:\n\nprompt_embeds → token-level conditioning (used in UNet cross-attention)\npooled_prompt_embeds → global conditioning (used via added_cond_kwargs)\n\nTogether, these embeddings fully represent the prompt in a format required for SDXL image generation.",
                      "lineHeight": 14,
                      "fontSize": 11,
                      "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
                    },
                    "lineHeight": 14,
                    "fontSize": 11,
                    "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
                  },
                  "lineHeight": 14,
                  "fontSize": 11,
                  "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
                },
                "lineHeight": 14,
                "fontSize": 11,
                "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
              },
              "lineHeight": 14,
              "fontSize": 11,
              "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
            },
            "lineHeight": 14,
            "fontSize": 11,
            "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
          },
          "lineHeight": 14,
          "fontSize": 11,
          "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
        },
        "lineHeight": 14,
        "fontSize": 11,
        "fontFamily": "-apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, \"Segoe UI\", Roboto, sans-serif"
      },
      "metadata": {
        "node_id": "node-clip-text-encode-(sdxl)-e87b6342",
        "name": "Clip Text Encode (SDXL)",
        "description": "This node converts a text prompt into the dual-embedding format required by Stable Diffusion XL by leveraging both CLIP text encoders (CLIP-L and CLIP BigG). Since both encoders and their tokenizers are already preloaded by the wrapper, the node focuses on tokenization, encoding, and combining outputs into SDXL-compatible conditioning tensors.\n\nThe prompt is independently tokenized using each encoder’s attached tokenizer, with padding and truncation to the standard 77-token length. These tokens are then passed through their respective text encoders under autocast for efficient mixed-precision inference.\n\nFor SDXL conditioning, the node extracts the penultimate hidden states from both encoders and concatenates them along the feature dimension to form the final prompt_embeds. This provides richer semantic understanding compared to single-encoder setups. Additionally, it extracts a pooled embedding (from the [CLS] token of the second encoder), which is used for global conditioning in the diffusion process.\n\nThe outputs are:\n\nprompt_embeds → token-level conditioning (used in UNet cross-attention)\npooled_prompt_embeds → global conditioning (used via added_cond_kwargs)\n\nTogether, these embeddings fully represent the prompt in a format required for SDXL image generation.",
